- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n![](images/6c/6cb2333688e1a4a255398262d43046554c817705f09f7be9d6e6d082af519c42.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate counterclockwise. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (counterclockwise), our thumb will point away from the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points away from the screen, in the direction of increasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133881 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d42243ea-58fc-53f6-a0b6-f995bf1d697c" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Curl of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "231945f5-5ce3-591d-8c60-6a81b26bce4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238364} {:answer/id #uuid "5cde9981-76e2-5111-b3d2-8d4bb59f9102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238866} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd04a75-4a7b-5127-a5de-42010f421aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238890} {:answer/id #uuid "e2dd249f-7c15-560d-becc-a4d5d9208c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240051} {:answer/id #uuid "f786ef25-6354-54b3-81ae-fc54056ba558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fd04a75-4a7b-5127-a5de-42010f421aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238890} :answer-field/id #uuid "5159a419-7fd4-51ac-aa59-e88ebd5af8fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6dbe3e81-bcac-55ba-b175-6059759ab5dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-105695" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cc62b0a51519230bd1230874c00e17f165c345d11bc64e6d3c8fcf7788a5ee8.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$\n- $F$ is not irrotational\n- $\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that our vector field $F$ represents the velocity field of a stream of flowing water. We place a paddlewheel at $P$ with the axis of rotation pointing in the $z$-direction, and we note whether the paddlewheel rotates or not.\n\n- If the paddlewheel does not rotate due to the action of the water, then the velocity field has zero curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F=0.$\n\n- If the paddlewheel is forced to rotate due to the action of the water, then the vector field has curl, i.e., $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n![](images/59/594106633e422d24b9fea113d24fb6367fdef5ac52724931860e4163e33ce992.png)\n\nIf $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ everywhere in ${R}^{3}$, we say that $F$ is irrotational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement II is true, whereas statement I is false. If a paddlewheel were placed at any point $P$ in the manner described above, it would rotate. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statements III is true. Since $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not irrotational.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Using our right hand, if we wrap our fingers in the direction of rotation (clockwise), our thumb will point into the screen. Therefore, $\\text{curl}\\,F$ also points into from the screen, in the direction of decreasing $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e40bb52-d447-5b63-8524-b2a531405e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241441} {:answer/id #uuid "9291f5e0-661c-5b67-b5f9-98411143c89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242466} {:answer/id #uuid "b5bc1ea3-bf67-5bbc-afec-5c54acd87a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242768} {:answer/id #uuid "d10a3eb1-dd7e-54ff-a306-219702a84f3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243022} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0d8c7b-86b5-51b3-a866-ce768aef7b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9291f5e0-661c-5b67-b5f9-98411143c89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242466} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd08eacf-dccc-58c1-bd33-7fea6c93187f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0880be83-dbd5-5dc8-a3cc-bdb045c80795" :question/math-academy-id "q-106684" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $0.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The  zero-matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $L.$\n- For any $A\\in L,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in L$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in L,$ we have $(A+B)-C=A+(B-C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|=0.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in L,$ there is a corresponding negative matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 0=0,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any matrices $A,B\\in L,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)-C=(A+B)+(-C)=A+(B+(-C))=A+(B-C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50e801e2-503a-559b-936f-f3458cb4c980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241902} {:answer/id #uuid "6c0376d8-916a-5c20-b820-b920ea81d4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242150} {:answer/id #uuid "c66a9c3d-3226-5038-8929-f96ac925e96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242932} {:answer/id #uuid "e35537bf-65b9-5ffa-a98b-f238d2931250" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243206} {:answer/id #uuid "f976c12a-1c69-5418-8d74-80ce87edd353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c66a9c3d-3226-5038-8929-f96ac925e96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242932} :answer-field/id #uuid "a62f5fb8-4988-5219-9bbb-89bef4000d32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2555255-85b1-529f-85fb-94b6f9684401" :question/math-academy-id "q-106688" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices with real entries and determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- The identity matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $S.$\n- For any $A\\in S,$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A\\in S$ such that $A+(-A)=O.$\n- There are matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ such that $(A+B)+C\\ne A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.$ Hence, $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any matrix $A\\in S,$ there is a corresponding negative $2\\times 2$ matrix $-A$ such that\n\n$$\nA+(-A)=A-A=O.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n|-A|=|-1\\cdot A|=(-1)^{2}\\cdot |A|=1\\cdot 1=1,\n$$\n\nhence $-A\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any matrices $A,B,C\\in S,$ we have\n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243926 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "487a4843-7c15-5ab1-8920-ea735dfdde90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241837} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7f17af-f142-53cc-8323-3a6aab476f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242005} {:answer/id #uuid "70a81903-8370-5b92-9e82-aeee09fbedcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242186} {:answer/id #uuid "8832c7a4-ead6-55c1-ba06-ea22126f602e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242375} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa223a8-d4f3-51ef-a918-0edfc6559919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b7f17af-f142-53cc-8323-3a6aab476f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242005} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6e7b59-87d4-567b-a3cf-d17339b0c4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f184570-b064-50c3-a82a-5d76cef8f98e" :question/math-academy-id "q-106700" :question/problem "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 \\\\ b & c\\end{aligned}],$ where $a,b,c\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\n- For any $A\\in M,$ we have $-A\\in M$ such that $A+(-A)=0.$\n- For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in M,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- For any $A,B,C\\in M,$ we have $(A+B)+C=A+(B+C).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\in M.$ Then $-A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the corresponding negative matrix such that $A+(-A)=O.$ However, $-{a}_{12}=-1\\ne 1,$ hence, $-A\\notin M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-matrix $O$ and any matrix $A\\in M,$ we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A\n$$\n\nsince $O$ is the unique zero element in the set of all matrices.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B,C\\in M,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n(A+B)+C=A+(B+C)\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5ab6fa-7f55-5ca4-b0fd-47c66f401c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241426} {:answer/id #uuid "68cb4930-5346-5250-b195-1320dc59351d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242121} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3cfbd3-7c51-51e1-a1cc-651adea01a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242288} {:answer/id #uuid "a3041743-ae01-5749-b3bd-e63042670543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242605} {:answer/id #uuid "af66c2ef-de66-52df-845a-a3736459f400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d5ab6fa-7f55-5ca4-b0fd-47c66f401c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241426} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b0d854e-3563-5ab5-a40f-3c0263e3318d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c35f03a5-0abf-5f24-a015-92c06eed08bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106703" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $10.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in T,$ we have $p(x)+0=0.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in T$, we have $p(x)+q(x)=p(x)-q(x).$\n- For any polynomial $p(x)\\in T$ we have $-p(x)\\in T$ such that $p(x)+(-p(x))=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}$ in $T.$ Then,\n\n$$\np(x)+0={x}^{2}+0={x}^{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=q(x)={x}^{2}\\in T.$\nThen:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x)+q(x) & ={x}^{2}+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ p(x)-q(x) & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\ne p(x)-q(x).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomial $p(x)\\in T,$ there is a corresponding negative polynomial $-p(x)$ such that\n\n$$\np(x)+(-p(x))=p(x)-p(x)=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(-p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))\\le 10,\n$$\n\nhence $-p(x)\\in T.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243932 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7041d5fd-aab1-57e4-9487-b5c9882af600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242180} {:answer/id #uuid "72850430-5595-545e-a2f2-2771edf7ffa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242195} {:answer/id #uuid "83fc1536-7b10-52d0-956e-7849f2977d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242335} {:answer/id #uuid "bbe59f91-6410-5a6d-8e3e-2fcdb03f1f4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242826} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa01516-17a5-5f65-a695-9c94fa04b033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83fc1536-7b10-52d0-956e-7849f2977d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242335} :answer-field/id #uuid "4127109b-95ff-5b82-9a34-cadc07c4058a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c34661b-de46-5e42-88de-7fe1ed369bf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-106710" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "701d17ff-50a7-5536-b4df-9571bad9e65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242179} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e58f8f-98c1-5566-bab6-056fbd747017" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242867} {:answer/id #uuid "e320c714-2212-553c-b439-46d43f5d3335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243203} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fa9ebe-6f7f-5b2b-bb7e-187d7fa94bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243240} {:answer/id #uuid "f6f628d1-2c72-5172-8e1f-d849b91df7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6f628d1-2c72-5172-8e1f-d849b91df7cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243363} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b1c32e1-ef3b-5c14-b5b5-31f0fc60dd9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae199b8f-96fc-541b-8e00-25e26e1c8f40" :question/math-academy-id "q-106751" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The second product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The third product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the right is not an upper triangular matrix, as it has a non-zero entry below the diagonal.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119853 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c1a562-1b3d-5180-9fef-e20ea916b17a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying LU Factorizations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e4281e7-8e52-5c7b-8f3d-2cb1b6fc51a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241589} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8a48ef-27b9-5d0b-b779-0a73997b77ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242280} {:answer/id #uuid "8d1b6ccf-a88f-5411-aaea-34ff5a293ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242425} {:answer/id #uuid "90fcd45e-86ab-5eba-8303-adc91481ddaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242450} {:answer/id #uuid "f73934b9-fcda-5b2c-ba39-aa57a7d4bc9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90fcd45e-86ab-5eba-8303-adc91481ddaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242450} :answer-field/id #uuid "0597f8f7-eb68-5031-85fe-6211cb52793b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02d764cc-1d89-57be-9543-a3cb1c6ba444" :question/math-academy-id "q-106756" :question/problem "Which of the following products is the LU factorization of a matrix $A?$\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]$\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that in the LU factorization of a matrix $A,$ we write $A=LU$ where the matrix $L$ is a unit lower triangular matrix (a lower triangular matrix that has only $1$'s on the main diagonal) and the matrix $U$ is an upper triangular matrix:\n\n$$\nA=\\overset{L}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{U}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each product in turn.\n\n- The first product is *not* in $LU$ form because the matrix on the left is not a unit lower triangular matrix, as its main diagonal contains a number not equal to $1.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- The second product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- The third product is in $LU$ form because the left matrix is a unit lower triangular matrix, and the right matrix is an upper triangular matrix.\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e469b5-07c6-584b-b3bd-1a166c6c0cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241327} {:answer/id #uuid "36663d6f-568a-5128-b12e-c1c00efc0541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241659} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3d6cce-e8b2-553a-80b1-192aefaf4bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241862} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd26119-c084-57ff-a50a-31cfe1d896f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241876} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d83713-39f9-5e61-ab77-6113890a9500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10e469b5-07c6-584b-b3bd-1a166c6c0cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241327} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8abf082-e05d-5b29-89ee-0c3cab9c4c33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d83ac51c-0c75-5956-b55e-b6c1972a1661" :question/math-academy-id "q-106809" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a|x|,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in N,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- For any $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider $f(x)=|x|\\in N.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot |x|=-|x|\\ne |x|.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)\\ne f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in N$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aea2174-dfeb-5a96-8044-c2efb4e92930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241278} {:answer/id #uuid "1f09035c-a73f-5bc0-b1e9-62489ebb423b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241452} {:answer/id #uuid "86070dcd-5d3e-5df7-96bd-50a77e905437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242352} {:answer/id #uuid "d059ad96-5ba2-538b-be61-12470c585a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243016} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5f3220-69a4-5a66-9699-c599ac5f66f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d059ad96-5ba2-538b-be61-12470c585a77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243016} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8d3769e-1942-5ecd-9ab4-4be4b985cc68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "253cf041-a7dc-53ad-a439-778e84d95095" :question/math-academy-id "q-106810" :question/problem "Let $J$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\frac{1}{x}\\in J$\n- For any $p(x)\\in J,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)\\in J$\n- For any $p(x),q(x)\\in J$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $J$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{x}\\notin J.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in J$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in J,$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03039dc6-1dfb-5fb8-9cd9-a75ee2ded12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241207} {:answer/id #uuid "054b883f-bba5-5e0c-a4b6-5ae661f7f6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241224} {:answer/id #uuid "1082defe-5e72-57fe-9385-7ab66130f25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241322} {:answer/id #uuid "ca9bf2a8-2d06-5f33-a19e-4f65b98a806b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242967} {:answer/id #uuid "e71f2082-95c7-5af8-a69a-50b89c1d1e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e71f2082-95c7-5af8-a69a-50b89c1d1e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243229} :answer-field/id #uuid "54287edb-3a24-5a97-817d-0ce7b6fd42c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43cc36f0-0278-53e6-b346-3f4ef69a0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-106812" :question/problem "Let $G$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- For any $f(x)\\in G,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- For any $f(x)\\in G$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).$\n- The scalar multiplication of functions in $G$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5400703c-4276-58f4-815d-1644689ab81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241933} {:answer/id #uuid "97996fa8-161b-52f6-a441-ae61b7403bfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242507} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d56f73-63ef-578a-a8ac-c36c016e2e17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242729} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d68249-32d7-5132-b5d6-fa6afd21bdae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243120} {:answer/id #uuid "ea09bc10-a189-51a4-ba5a-91dbe0cd1650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea09bc10-a189-51a4-ba5a-91dbe0cd1650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243252} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b7890c2-dd59-5b6e-9c28-f5f43fd71229" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c2f74bb-ddf4-5d4c-b244-ad5b6a694598" :question/math-academy-id "q-106897" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $10?$\n\n- ${R}_{10}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $10$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{5}(R)$, the vector space of all $5\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{10}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{10}[t])=10+1=11\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(\\text{R}))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{5}(R))={5}^{2}=25\\ne 10.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{10})=10.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21502600-df87-5967-ae24-50099bbb8ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241470} {:answer/id #uuid "52226d51-1e13-502e-89a3-6004bfbd35ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241912} {:answer/id #uuid "8af89d56-a1e3-5d8e-ab76-776d37ed95b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242407} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5077e1-5620-5715-9fe1-0aaefcb7d12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242539} {:answer/id #uuid "c97549a5-cbf7-5e38-b461-0835a6fc8843" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242954}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8af89d56-a1e3-5d8e-ab76-776d37ed95b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242407} :answer-field/id #uuid "648b36cb-7990-5600-a252-926a6c35b12e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf92f8df-b777-508f-9358-1b7029534cba" :question/math-academy-id "q-106900" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $3?$\n\n- ${R}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{2}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{3}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 3$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{2})=2\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{2}[t])=2+1=3.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3}(R))={3}^{2}=9\\ne 3.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aee17e3a-031d-5c0e-9488-17410b3a2372" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Dimensions of Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e3a45a8-6953-50e1-af6c-bea57c5038ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241440} {:answer/id #uuid "6e45167d-32f9-58fe-8215-ec166d4699cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242164} {:answer/id #uuid "c435e16d-bae6-5f9e-9084-6216b51cb9f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242904} {:answer/id #uuid "df285cac-721d-54e1-9bf9-a8647dee1d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243164} {:answer/id #uuid "efd7e09f-760a-5771-b745-08a5c5c5607c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e3a45a8-6953-50e1-af6c-bea57c5038ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241440} :answer-field/id #uuid "c93f0280-1245-5a4a-920a-a7e04c1f8297" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "19d4d0ee-ad5b-53e7-956e-b02eb6cf177b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106901" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $20?$\n\n- ${R}_{20}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 5$ matrices over $R$\n- ${R}^{20}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21\\ne 20.\\,\\times \n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 5}(R))=4\\cdot 5=20.\\,✓\n$$\n- Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{20})=20.\\,✓\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b777c1d-0987-5ddd-931c-b5cdab08c06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241860} {:answer/id #uuid "98cbd8e1-0c5a-50e3-88e9-eb053a002ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242514} {:answer/id #uuid "aadcba4c-5d85-54a3-88f2-76e7019622d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242673} {:answer/id #uuid "b80e65bc-d140-55cb-ae2a-d60f44d2c09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242795} {:answer/id #uuid "e98fd405-e829-5706-a855-22b44ca5759f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aadcba4c-5d85-54a3-88f2-76e7019622d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242673} :answer-field/id #uuid "85fda121-0c7d-56ff-a307-5d708dc554fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28ab3efd-4668-556c-a241-6c3f64cf9ac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-106916" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the absolute values of the terms are finite\n- ${R}_{3000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3\\,000$\n- ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 60$ matrices over $R$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in {1,2,…,n},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{1}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{3000}[t]$ and  ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{3000}[t])=3\\,000+1=3\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R))=50\\cdot 60=3\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21e27a77-17e4-5b05-b8b5-42ddb50a3663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241473} {:answer/id #uuid "338251e4-d90f-50e0-b7e1-23d7e28826c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241634} {:answer/id #uuid "467f0cad-0f65-5c2a-a7dd-e55f70fc3b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241817} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9a7045-01d4-5909-9fff-f5c6752fec27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242016} {:answer/id #uuid "f4501265-2c93-55db-a331-1380629939f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c9a7045-01d4-5909-9fff-f5c6752fec27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242016} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff9c9993-a272-54fc-b7cd-ff51bde6faba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d584308b-6723-5e86-a9ec-6146a097d3d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-107042" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly dependent and ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ has a non-zero solution\n- ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$\n- ${v}_{3}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is a linearly dependent set, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0\n$$\n\nhas a non-zero solution.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, solving equation ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}+{x}_{4}{v}_{4}=0$ for ${v}_{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{v}_{4}=-\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{2}-\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{4}}{v}_{3}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, ${x}_{4}$ can't be zero because then\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nwill have a non-zero solution, which contradicts the fact that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\nHence, ${v}_{4}$ is a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent set, the equation \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nhas no non-zero solution. Hence, ${v}_{3}$ is not a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cd92405-02c6-5a62-b8b5-c0adc23129d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242157} {:answer/id #uuid "8e744672-b586-517b-b695-2c0349dfe2d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242432} {:answer/id #uuid "adbfe95b-b76c-5d99-948c-1a376b43ff69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242698} {:answer/id #uuid "b9613222-8a5b-5a7a-b79e-e341b2f7bdab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242805} {:answer/id #uuid "d41f0180-5fdd-5302-b5d0-55961dd132a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cd92405-02c6-5a62-b8b5-c0adc23129d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242157} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55f8088-4d40-5543-b0fb-123e13d3f497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4ec52379-6b69-5ec0-af4a-1a5b49cab942" :question/math-academy-id "q-107043" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}+{v}_{3},2{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n0\\cdot {v}_{1}+a\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {v}_{1}+1\\cdot {v}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot ({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222b789c-0257-5c2a-8c82-1ee5269a4d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241476} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7b40d3-fcda-5ecf-9c92-3c6c218bb18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242831} {:answer/id #uuid "c7060f10-ae7a-5fea-8b08-3a056ded01ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242939} {:answer/id #uuid "d9890a30-c73d-5093-b06f-472427379792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243116} {:answer/id #uuid "e89bd240-441e-5e1c-bf22-e77a404985a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "222b789c-0257-5c2a-8c82-1ee5269a4d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241476} :answer-field/id #uuid "372644fd-41a7-5d60-8044-2e3b5f1533d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7243a9b-252c-5b36-9021-bf96acea16e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-107044" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{3},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}}$ is linearly  independent\n- ${0,{v}_{1},-{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{3})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}-({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot (-{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}0+{x}_{2}{v}_{1}+{x}_{3}(-{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${0,{v}_{1},-{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$  is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "744ac182-5331-5399-ac3f-a45616618736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242212} {:answer/id #uuid "a353f784-6dc7-5793-99a1-9934ad655646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242610} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cd0903-b1cd-531a-8dbc-6ce05e0f83e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242878} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb8e2e5-274d-5836-9ead-f357143e0d8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243299} {:answer/id #uuid "ef86df2e-b21b-58b7-9537-78f29395fa6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a353f784-6dc7-5793-99a1-9934ad655646" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242610} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dfb5cb8-49f4-5b8c-871f-2198fcf107a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24ab3e85-383c-51f5-887f-f697f1697031" :question/math-academy-id "q-107045" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},2{v}_{2}-{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}(2{v}_{2}-{v}_{1})+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){v}_{1}+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{2{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},2{v}_{2}-{v}_{1},{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot (-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{v}_{1})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},-{v}_{1}}$  is linearly dependent. In general, any set containing the zero vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Indeed,  if this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+0\\cdot {v}_{2}+b\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124485 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Given Set Is Linearly Independent Given Information on Its Elements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0294268a-ffaa-5520-8c92-07df70d529e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241205} {:answer/id #uuid "31281f32-d780-5a37-a753-3c2296df564f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241616} {:answer/id #uuid "41615e68-b2d7-5ccc-861a-5869eea99b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241766} {:answer/id #uuid "84a3e361-7151-5b36-98ea-7398355861d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242340} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c7d678-217f-51e6-b859-c0e49ebb4f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84a3e361-7151-5b36-98ea-7398355861d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242340} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7fd45e-b6b0-5903-83c6-bbe887fbcc07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "931e27f9-e2c3-59ee-b477-76a2f89436c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-107048" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${2{v}_{1},-3{v}_{2},4{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${-{v}_{1},-{v}_{2},-{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}(-3{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(4{v}_{3}) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+(-3{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+(4{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{2{v}_{1},-3{v}_{2},4{v}_{3}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n(-2)\\cdot {v}_{1}+3\\cdot {v}_{2}+1\\cdot (2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}(2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},2{v}_{1}-3{v}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, suppose\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-{v}_{1})+{x}_{2}(-{v}_{2})+{x}_{3}(-{v}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, implies that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-{x}_{1}){v}_{1}+(-{x}_{2}){v}_{2}+(-{x}_{3}){v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,  since ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, it\nmust be ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$  Therefore, the set \n${-{v}_{1},-{v}_{2},-{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124591 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b1bb974-826a-590f-8d32-cdc868ca827c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Polynomial Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a60d388-bc83-5af7-8225-3508acff2075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241407} {:answer/id #uuid "486f34a6-fce7-59c0-b718-04843afaa8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241836} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4f8115-6911-5b89-ae5f-2e6c858659ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242272} {:answer/id #uuid "d742eee0-3a02-5298-9b21-8bd95e26bb86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243090} {:answer/id #uuid "e42fe48a-c999-5f78-b148-00f5fd4a9bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a60d388-bc83-5af7-8225-3508acff2075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241407} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a253ae0-6c8f-5b4c-b09b-a9105706149e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3056b610-3deb-5e11-aff8-96abed8c674b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107102" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5}+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}{t}^{5}+{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}+b$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{5}+b) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5}+(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d872b22-7cba-5036-8273-9a20efcc7e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241729} {:answer/id #uuid "78c2ab8b-9c2d-551d-8848-61255645e2a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242245} {:answer/id #uuid "7c438634-2ca9-5de8-a418-b73be0ed3bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242270} {:answer/id #uuid "eec86e1b-cd5f-56da-9600-e976237c1328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243300} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfa47ed-d690-5ca9-ab1b-37e40f90f69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eec86e1b-cd5f-56da-9600-e976237c1328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243300} :answer-field/id #uuid "0be788ee-6d06-5deb-86a5-8c46bba98ee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f27a4a5-4686-5577-a411-11aa97fab74a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107112" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $C={[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a\\in R}.$ \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $C$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $C$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $C$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{a}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $C$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & -a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}ka & 0 \\\\ 0 & -ka\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $C.$ So, $C$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $C$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a55fd99-cc21-54f6-9f21-32f483b49ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241272} {:answer/id #uuid "195ea848-9a4e-54b7-bc92-80ae80187e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241399} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad960d0-30c8-5b7c-9750-0570f53aed4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242001} {:answer/id #uuid "ac58dc68-ab41-5333-9612-568acb1da65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242687} {:answer/id #uuid "d068d781-d242-5a93-af8b-2d493319de3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ad960d0-30c8-5b7c-9750-0570f53aed4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242001} :answer-field/id #uuid "b08e5c99-11cc-5ab5-b8be-9ab5b73b56f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89286e95-724b-57e9-aee8-7d71e484cafb" :question/math-academy-id "q-107113" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R$ and its subset $A={[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,|\\,a,b\\in R}.$  \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $A$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $A,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1} & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & {a}_{1}+{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{aligned}]$ is an element of $A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kA & =k[\\begin{aligned}0 & a \\\\ b & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & ka \\\\ kb & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a matrix from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "230a6e01-18bc-58d0-8387-1d66163f631b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241484} {:answer/id #uuid "426b663e-f591-5196-99d7-5a2d5500a45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241777} {:answer/id #uuid "4db4b11e-cc15-5b26-8274-c5279317ce7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241875} {:answer/id #uuid "504d1f79-8308-5f06-a1aa-17458982e5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241897} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ef2203-b098-59db-9ad6-e52a17c8c5f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504d1f79-8308-5f06-a1aa-17458982e5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241897} :answer-field/id #uuid "9183d31f-6b62-5ef0-8cb0-c498d066f789" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72b2f218-4463-5051-8c3b-5d02162b5905" :question/math-academy-id "q-107115" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $D$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices such that the sum of all its entries equals $0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $D$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $D$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {d}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {d}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $D.$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2} & {d}_{1}+{d}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2})+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})+({d}_{1}+{d}_{2}) & = \\\\ ({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1})+({a}_{1}+{b}_{1}+{c}_{1}+{d}_{1}) & = \\\\ 0+0 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that ${A}_{1}+{A}_{2}$ also belongs to $D.$ So, $D$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\in D$ and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ \\alpha c & \\alpha d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n and so\nthe sum of the entries of $\\alpha A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a+\\alpha b+\\alpha c+\\alpha d & =\\alpha (a+b+c+d) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $D$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $D$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124590 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8f306cf2-6881-5948-90bd-4e0ce98b4116" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Matrix Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50a208c6-34bf-520c-81bc-1c8293d73759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241900} {:answer/id #uuid "60b1b0ce-241e-57de-bbd8-fd74eb2dc23c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242049} {:answer/id #uuid "7874aacf-c573-5a68-b713-b24449852cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242241} {:answer/id #uuid "c45adc05-64d4-5d99-839e-ad37dc03a079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242908} {:answer/id #uuid "d835a3fb-965a-563d-9127-d900d25d31ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50a208c6-34bf-520c-81bc-1c8293d73759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6c23cbe-3714-5f55-acd5-0f4a19ef1a32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3b977ec-88a0-5b97-96bf-4f197129ba2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107119" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R,$  and its subset $T$ containing all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T$ is closed under matrix addition\n- $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $T$ is a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $T$ is **not** a subspace of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If ${A}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {b}_{1} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ are two matrices from $T,$  then \n\n$$\n{A}_{1}+{A}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & {b}_{1}+{b}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nis also an element of $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, if $A=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ 0 & c\\end{aligned}]\\in T$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\alpha A=[\\begin{aligned}\\alpha a & \\alpha b \\\\ 0 & \\alpha c\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nalso belongs to $T.$ So, $T$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $T$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2588e089-4786-5346-b42d-65c2740ae2ab" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Polynomial Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a74844-13bd-593e-99ab-4f9dc151d2ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241524} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c34971-a13e-5098-aa51-ca5b8bb3c244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242652} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa3ec-1ba8-51fb-b08c-5e0b7b2c95a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243039} {:answer/id #uuid "d91da20c-4f2b-5dc4-bca6-039d62d36a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243111} {:answer/id #uuid "ff284b33-0629-565a-bd48-d911095443c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dfa3ec-1ba8-51fb-b08c-5e0b7b2c95a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243039} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eba7ddb-ab05-5220-a1f6-52a824d383b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38344b2a-4c7e-5844-831c-94e852b34e5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-107131" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ over $R$ with degrees at most $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a linearly independent set in ${R}_{2}[t]$\n- $2-t$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${0,1,t}$\n- ${0,1,t}$ is a basis of ${R}_{2}[t]$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot t=0,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-vector is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n2-t=0\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot t.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c65e49f-e63b-520e-a8d8-1e64704afdf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241717} {:answer/id #uuid "ac59755b-369e-5e18-80e8-907aa3ebb11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242688} {:answer/id #uuid "b855b893-85c7-565f-8791-cc779d46a7ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I,II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242798} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd72eee-922b-5b5e-9443-e97544e5996c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242837} {:answer/id #uuid "f59267b4-aa62-5bfd-b574-01d1a5f291e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac59755b-369e-5e18-80e8-907aa3ebb11f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242688} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d81c556-c49b-59ba-8c07-d213256d2da3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eace6f7a-724d-598c-b716-132ee8202185" :question/math-academy-id "q-107137" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =6.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=5v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=5{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $5$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{5}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{6}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06018602-8f20-5e46-b8f1-ec60b62415c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241231} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef4b0bd-56c6-538d-ab62-c3ca16878682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241306} {:answer/id #uuid "3d70afbc-3a63-51cf-ba1c-d7579c6c413a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241728} {:answer/id #uuid "93da9f44-fdb7-5ebb-9ee9-46862efef29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242477} {:answer/id #uuid "a1252964-96da-5719-b42a-5b3be0c79793" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II  only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93da9f44-fdb7-5ebb-9ee9-46862efef29e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242477} :answer-field/id #uuid "65afd75d-9f84-5f07-a4b8-dbf9039b6bba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d7a7e364-e908-54cb-a453-163231b198f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-107139" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $N$ containing all functions such that $f(0)=0.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $N$ is closed under function addition\n- $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $N$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $N$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $N.$ Then $f(x)=g(x)=0$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)(0) & =f(0)+g(0) \\\\  & =0+0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under function addition.\n\n- Statement II is true.  Indeed, suppose $f(x)\\in N$  and let $\\alpha \\in R.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha f)(0) & =\\alpha \\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =\\alpha \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $N$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement IV is false. $N$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d4e378a-86ef-5a01-91ea-9154881076fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241874} {:answer/id #uuid "7c705c5a-285d-53ad-8486-e12ad0a6a4f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242274} {:answer/id #uuid "8b8c5f9c-2d1f-5da8-bcdf-40e16a6fe181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242415} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a424b7-ed1b-55fd-a7a8-9c6ca5776f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243062} {:answer/id #uuid "f1e210c5-19e4-5d18-b820-1f58ace4cd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e378a-86ef-5a01-91ea-9154881076fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241874} :answer-field/id #uuid "707f3600-9de3-5ff3-b345-2eba81f53fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3ebe5cf-3b69-5829-b6a6-d075f8831709" :question/math-academy-id "q-107141" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $W$ containing all polynomials of degree greater than $2.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $W$ is closed under function addition\n- $W$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $W$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $W$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}+1$ and $g(x)=-{x}^{3}+1,$ then\n\n$$\nf(x)+g(x)={x}^{3}+1+(-{x}^{3})+1=2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial of degree zero. So, $W$ is *not* closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $k=0,$ then\n\n$$\nkf(x)=0\\cdot {x}^{3}=0.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $W$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $W$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244035 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124488 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef65513d-1e9e-571f-8b93-4e2d63aed68e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Subspaces of Function Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e03976e-08a6-5648-ae7d-b8408176b5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241437} {:answer/id #uuid "6448cf7a-2122-5687-a2f2-8abc1fbf0cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242082} {:answer/id #uuid "8184bde3-685a-5aa4-b0dd-04ebb9ce9687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242314} {:answer/id #uuid "d48eb932-44cc-5a49-86db-aba41c5c0503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243061} {:answer/id #uuid "ec861a27-2c70-5732-bd70-ff1420c5a451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6448cf7a-2122-5687-a2f2-8abc1fbf0cc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242082} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddaf70c-8377-50f0-9c4a-933b09df9474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "997e8053-09a0-5e0a-853a-5f7c609b31ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-107143" :question/problem "Consider the vector space $C[0,1]$ of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$ and its subset $M$ containing all functions that return an integer value at $x=1.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $M$ is closed under function addition\n- $M$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $M$ is a subspace of $C[0,1]$\n- $M$ is **not** a subspace of $C[0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, if $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are functions from $M,$ then $f(1)$  and  $g(1)$ are integers and so $(f+g)(1)=f(1)+g(1)$ must be an integer number too. So, $M$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, $f(x)=1$ is a function in $M$, but $0.5f(x)$ is not in $M$ because\n$(0.5f)(1)=0.5\\cdot f(1)=0.5$ is not an integer. So, $M$ is *not*  closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $M$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d8dad9f-813f-5ee0-8b56-4e213fbd9e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241584} {:answer/id #uuid "b610900a-7758-5500-abbe-f65a94a26875" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242772} {:answer/id #uuid "b62e3fb1-f541-583d-b7dd-fcea40cef7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242775} {:answer/id #uuid "cf4ba2f8-bab2-5a0b-bac1-77232870019d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243009} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dffa3a-5847-5b0c-8e10-435f8b6ecf88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2dffa3a-5847-5b0c-8e10-435f8b6ecf88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243040} :answer-field/id #uuid "463fc030-fafe-5c38-871c-9424a7e4d0ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243571}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "580e535d-24ca-5116-ba35-64ccc5c2ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107314" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{7}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =7.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{7}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{7}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=7v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(\\frac{1}{2}v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10d9e9eb-bd2b-53b9-908b-3cefdc2f7abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241324} {:answer/id #uuid "5962710f-f671-5ebf-848b-bf1ec4b3852d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241985} {:answer/id #uuid "d94b4f70-6a9e-5e47-a422-9232a86505e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243113} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e3ebbb-ecbf-5b41-9468-b137f8adf22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243211} {:answer/id #uuid "ec2d2b65-8257-5f91-8d5b-5a6347330d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec2d2b65-8257-5f91-8d5b-5a6347330d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243269} :answer-field/id #uuid "162e4ac8-ff20-5c93-b906-61516bd643aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "265a681e-3e0d-553b-975a-40472b8deb57" :question/math-academy-id "q-107347" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere the first coefficient is nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent. In general, any set that contains a zero-matrix is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]+(-3)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f733f0d-7e29-56f9-9ebf-5a85bfa39026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241457} {:answer/id #uuid "7fecbffa-150f-5edd-b337-e26de5eb732a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242302} {:answer/id #uuid "a5bfaec9-dba4-5628-a71c-4931987e5008" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242637} {:answer/id #uuid "b61ce3ba-080b-5ff6-8f0a-29dd16a73603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242773} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b01a2c-55b7-59eb-882b-87a2a49d3b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2b01a2c-55b7-59eb-882b-87a2a49d3b01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242888} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc0d681c-0891-5641-b651-e58caface354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "727e9706-00b1-50a3-b21c-2eb79073afed" :question/math-academy-id "q-107534" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]=2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+b\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a & -a \\\\ 0 & 2b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124489 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "073042ee-d96d-5872-9681-ddcea43a2d37" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Sets of Vectors in a Matrix Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bcfec00-ddba-56d0-891f-ec779c705f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242009} {:answer/id #uuid "8e096644-cddd-5be1-bc7d-ddeed9e4ab6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242429} {:answer/id #uuid "9c826cad-2051-56d1-95c1-7fca5e3a70f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242547} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd5c2d7-feb7-5fc0-8f91-227b428c2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243170} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e7c716-faf0-5a7c-94b5-5525a548d2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfd5c2d7-feb7-5fc0-8f91-227b428c2cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243170} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ce8875-c21e-50de-b4c8-8746ee90ff05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3a9f3d6-7047-5a33-b81e-ab3641786305" :question/math-academy-id "q-107536" :question/problem "Consider the vector space ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ of all $2\\times 2$ matrices over $R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$\n- $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- ${[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere some coefficients are nonzero, so the set is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]+5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the set is not linearly independent, it can't form a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2619addf-a4eb-5abe-a3f0-58ff32c18c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241510} {:answer/id #uuid "394402fa-e5dc-545e-83dc-f87e15f003a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241692} {:answer/id #uuid "4d10664d-6704-5141-9beb-d2bf289242ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241870} {:answer/id #uuid "69c85ab3-3852-52c8-a0ea-63c45aa500d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242130} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f71ada-1b72-5c61-8692-2c9384b9560b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2619addf-a4eb-5abe-a3f0-58ff32c18c62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241510} :answer-field/id #uuid "68a2a4e9-260a-589e-85e8-521a45a0ca09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d745992-cf32-533c-af78-280d0db594aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107587" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains a vector from another vector space.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{2}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}$ spans $V$ since any linear combination of vectors from $C$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{1}+{c}_{3}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3}=({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}({c}_{1}+{c}_{3})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244094 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b86c8ce-b8e9-570a-baf5-6350efd57a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241282} {:answer/id #uuid "69db45df-8924-5a9f-b27e-f9864533f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242131} {:answer/id #uuid "90a38045-6eab-56a2-bb7f-87c9f2fe114b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242445} {:answer/id #uuid "c881833e-3338-5654-b8e5-23ee253cf2b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242947} {:answer/id #uuid "fc84dbfc-e148-5b66-bae5-f923459ee483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc84dbfc-e148-5b66-bae5-f923459ee483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243406} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2bb7e5e-91d1-5c16-88c1-922128ced4b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "013a3d9d-c3ab-5357-a425-3057635e56b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-107588" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$, any vector of ${R}^{3},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({b}_{1})+{x}_{2}(2{b}_{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+(2{x}_{2}){b}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, since ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}$ spans ${R}^{2}$ since any linear combination of $B$ can be expressed as a linear combination of ${{b}_{1},2{b}_{2}}:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{b}_{1}+{x}_{2}{b}_{2}={x}_{1}{b}_{1}+\\frac{{x}_{2}}{2}(2{b}_{2})\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},2{c}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the third vector is a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that the set is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124490 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca956d3e-37a8-5f0f-a062-97d0567d54e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Bases of Abstract Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862675ad-652d-5e86-82e9-c1d292bd2aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242354} {:answer/id #uuid "95b31b4b-6eb3-55f2-b875-e84600a1da73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242494} {:answer/id #uuid "a14c2dbd-c68a-517b-b3b7-0e486c3200c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242591} {:answer/id #uuid "e4d039b4-18bb-544f-8f39-02c2e98293d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243215} {:answer/id #uuid "fc28a3d0-d51f-535b-9787-87270ae4ca5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a14c2dbd-c68a-517b-b3b7-0e486c3200c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242591} :answer-field/id #uuid "455a0965-9cf6-5f4f-8b5b-f7c43300126a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fba7ca1e-918b-5610-87e4-9553b3a87d7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-107589" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}$ and ${R}^{2}$, respectively. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$\n- ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B$ and $C$ are bases, the following properties must hold:\n\n- $B$ and $C$ are maximally linearly independent sets in ${R}^{2}$ and ${R}^{3},$ respectively, and\n\n- $B$ spans ${R}^{2},$ while $C$ spans ${R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$ is a basis of ${R}^{2}$, any vector of ${R}^{2},$ including the given vector $x,$ can be written as a linear combination of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This means that ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2},x}$ is linearly dependent and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the vector ${b}_{3}$ is in ${R}^{3}$ but it is not a linear combination of  ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ as the set  ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. This means that the set does not span ${R}^{3}$ and therefore, cannot be a basis.\n\n- Statement III is false. The set ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{c}_{1}}$ is not a basis of ${R}^{3}$ because the set contains vectors from another vector space.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."}]
